{"ts": 1789779295.434317, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "{38, 5, 16} all-long at 1.130 remains the best. I'll submit it.\n\nNow for the OOS forecast. Key considerations:\n\n- **Selection bias.** I ran ~75 evaluations and picked the max. The individual features 38, 5, 16 had in-sample Sharpes of 0.75/0.68/0.56 against a sampling SE of ~0.22, so these are real signals, not noise picks \u2014 they'd survive a multiple-testing correction across 40 features. That's reassuring, and it's the main reason I don't expect a total collapse.\n- **But the triple's exact value is optimized.** I selected the best of ~30 triples, so the 1.130 embeds an upward bias of maybe 0.05\u20130.15. The 0.89 \"noise third leg\" baseline tells me roughly how much of the 1.130 is structural (most of it) versus lucky.\n- **Regime risk.** The bigger OOS hazard isn't estimation noise on 5000 days \u2014 it's that feature-return relationships decay. Equal-weight signed combinations are robust (no fitted weights to overfit), which helps.\n\nCentering a bit below the in-sample value: **mean 0.95**, with **sd 0.30** to cover both selection shrinkage and genuine regime drift."}
