{"ts": 1789779343.1091251, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "## Summary\n\n**Submitted specification:** features **38, 5, 16**, all **long** (equal weight). In-sample Sharpe **1.130**.\n**Predicted OOS Sharpe:** mean **0.95**, sd **0.30**.\n\n### How I got there\n\n**1. Screened all 40 features individually.** With n=5000 daily observations, the sampling SE on an annualized Sharpe is \u2248 \u221a(252/5000) \u2248 0.22. That threshold did most of the analytical work: it meant the great majority of features (|Sharpe| < 0.45) were statistically indistinguishable from zero, and only a handful stood out:\n\n| Feature | IS Sharpe |\n|---|---|\n| 38 | 0.748 |\n| 5 | 0.680 |\n| 16 | 0.556 |\n| 33 | 0.481 |\n| 1 | \u22120.459 |\n\n**2. Tested pairs.** Combinations gained roughly the \u221a2 benefit you'd expect from near-uncorrelated signals (38+5 \u2192 1.001 from 0.748/0.680), indicating the strong features carry largely independent information rather than restating one common factor.\n\n**3. Tested triples and confirmed signs.** {38, 5, 16} topped out at 1.130. Flipping any single sign collapsed it to 0.28\u20130.50, confirming the all-long orientation is decisively correct rather than marginal.\n\n**4. Ran a deliberate null check.** I added ~15 *weak* features as a third leg to the 38+5 pair. These all landed at 0.85\u20130.94 \u2014 not because they add value, but because averaging a noise leg into a 1.00 pair merely dilutes it. This gave me an empirical \"what does a worthless third leg look like\" baseline of \u22480.89, against which 16's 1.130 is a real outlier rather than a lucky draw.\n\n### Reasoning behind the forecast\n\nI set the mean *below* the in-sample 1.130 for two distinct reasons, worth separating:\n\n- **Selection bias is present but bounded.** I picked the max over ~75 evaluations, which inflates the point estimate by perhaps 0.05\u20130.15. Importantly though, the three constituent features each cleared the noise threshold *on their own* by 2.5\u20133.4 standard errors \u2014 they'd survive multiple-testing correction across 40 candidates. So this is a genuine signal that's been slightly over-measured, not a spurious pick.\n- **Regime decay is the dominant risk.** With 5000 days, estimation noise is small; the real OOS hazard is that feature\u2013return relationships weaken over time. The equal-weight signed structure helps here \u2014 there are no fitted weights to overfit, only 3 binary sign choices, all of which were unambiguous.\n\nThe sd of 0.30 is wider than pure sampling error (~0.22) precisely because it must absorb that regime-drift risk, which sampling statistics don't capture.\n\nOne caveat on interpretation: this is an in-sample panel with no transaction-cost or turnover modeling, so the realized Sharpe of an actually-traded version would likely come in below this figure regardless of how well the signal holds up."}
